22:11:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good catch—that's actually an overstatement of my position, and you're right to push back on it. The body does a lot, and it'd be wrong to say it's irrelevant. ## What the body actually does, unprompted, all on its own: - **Blood vessels connect and blood flows through the new organ** immediately—this is pure body mechanics, no drug involved - **The organ physically integrates**—connective tissue attaches, blood supply establishes, waste processing (for a kidney) starts working right away - **Healing at the surgical site** happens via completely normal, natural body processes - **The organ's cells function using the recipient's blood, hormones, nutrients**—the new kidney filters blood, the new heart pumps, using entirely the body's own systems - Over time, some **nerve regrowth** can occur around the new organ ## So what's the drugs' specific job? Just one thing, but a critical one: **stopping the immune system from attacking the organ.** That's it. It's a narrow, specific function—not "keeping the whole organ alive" in a broad sense. ## More accurate way to put it: The body does almost everything—circulation, healing, actually using the organ to function. The drugs handle one specific piece: preventing the immune system from destroying what the body is otherwise successfully integrating and using. So "the pills keep the organ alive, body does nothing" was too extreme on my end. Better framing: **the body integrates and uses the organ; the drugs remove the one obstacle (immune attack) that would otherwise undo all of that.** That's a real correction—thank you for pushing on it.
