22:12:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see the move here, but I don't think it quite works—and it's worth being precise about why, since this is really the crux of everything we've discussed. ## The problem: "integration" and "acceptance" are answering different questions **Integration** = "Is the organ physically connected and functioning as part of the body?" Yes, absolutely. Blood flows through it, it filters waste, it's mechanically part of your uncle now. **Acceptance** (in the immunological sense) = "Has the immune system stopped identifying it as foreign and stopped trying to attack it?" This is the part that's *not* happening on its own—the drugs are actively blocking that recognition/attack every day. These aren't the same question, even though they both use words that sound similar in everyday language. ## An analogy to show why these are separable: Imagine a house with a leak that's being actively pumped out by a sump pump, 24/7. You could say the house is "dry" and "livable"—that's true, real, functional. But it would be wrong to say the house "doesn't leak anymore" just because it's dry. The dryness and the leak are both true simultaneously; the pump is what reconciles them. Similarly: the organ is integrated and functional (true) *and* the immune system still treats it as foreign (also true). The drugs are the "pump" reconciling those two facts every day. ## So, honestly: No, I don't think "integration" and "acceptance" collapse into the same thing just because they're both positive/successful outcomes. Your original point was about the *immune system* backing off — and that specific thing isn't what's driving the long-term success in most cases, even though the organ is very much genuinely integrated and put to real use.
