22:12:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely good challenge, and there's real asymmetry to explain here—not just "this one's different because I say so." ## The key difference: "cured" is actually accurate; ongoing "accepted" isn't quite When a doctor says "the medication cured the infection," that statement is **fully true and complete** — the infection is gone, treatment is done, nothing ongoing is required. The word "cured" correctly implies finality, and that's exactly what happened. With transplants, "the body accepted the organ" *implies* a similar finality — that something changed permanently and no more intervention is needed. But that's the one part that isn't true for most patients. The medication isn't finished; it's still running, every day, indefinitely. So the words "accepted" (implying done/permanent) and the actual situation (ongoing/continuous) don't match up the way "cured" and "no more infection" do. ## A closer parallel would be: "The medication is controlling my blood pressure" — nobody says "my body accepted normal blood pressure" and then stops their meds, because everyone intuitively understands hypertension medication is ongoing management, not a cure. That phrasing naturally implies continuation. The issue isn't that transplant mechanisms deserve some special lecture that infections don't. It's that "cured" happens to be accurate shorthand for infections, while "accepted" (as commonly used) happens to imply something false for transplants — permanence — when the real situation is closer to "controlled" or "managed," ongoing. So you're right that doctors don't over-explain most things, and they shouldn't have to. This one just happens to be a case where the everyday phrase quietly implies the wrong idea (finality) about an ongoing process — not because transplants are special, but because that particular word choice created a mismatch that "cured" doesn't.
